      },
      "lineNbr": 54,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-02T15:32:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I double checked in gcc, and same basic outcome: explicit ternary is branchy, `std::min` is not. The branchless `std::min` in GCC is implemented in a very surprising way; it looks like it is doing a \"compute both sides, then do an `if_then_else`-style masked combine\" which is way less efficient than using the min opcode directly!",
      "parentUuid": "02856d87_47c28524",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 54,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 54,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "04f9ba6904679e175e89c135d5fb360905d4b3df",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5a7cb046_c9d0a546",
        "filename": "src/opts/SkSwizzler_opts.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 54,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-02T15:36:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I also noticed the std::round calls. We can definitely look at improving that (in a follow-up, or before landing) - given that this doesn\u0027t need to handle arbitrary values, I think we\u0027ll get away with a simple addition of 0.5, then convert to int?\n\nThe branchiness is annoying, because I don\u0027t love the impact on a\u003d0 pixels (those will almost always have RGB\u003d0, so the result would still be transparent black - maybe we try that way. The only thing that comes to mind where we\u0027d have non-zero RGB with zero A is from a runtime shader or similar, where the user is explicitly overriding our normal behavior.",
      "parentUuid": "02856d87_47c28524",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 54,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 54,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "04f9ba6904679e175e89c135d5fb360905d4b3df",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
